FINDINGS (Full) #C1018+#C1035 one-checker-per-tip 746f8c107fed7d87f2d1010c03001d49b67d3818: two MEDIUM, one LOW. Back to Boardsmith.
Suite: npm test 2864/2864 pass, exit 0. Own mutants X1-X8 (takeover of a held tip, loser told ok, dispatch filter off, another signer's note replaced, origin not checked, re-cut taken, foreign release, noted tip claimed): all killed.

F1 MEDIUM scripts/sign-note.mjs:31-38: --push fails in the two-signer case it exists for. It fetches origin's notes into a private ref only to check them, then adds the note on the local refs/notes/checker, which is behind whenever the other checker pushed a note on ANY commit since. Probe (temp bare repo, two clones): A signs s1 and pushes; B signs s3 and pushes; A signs s2 with --push -> "the note is written but the push failed" (non-fast-forward), origin has no note on s2, and A's local ref has diverged. Fix: write the note on top of origin's ref (e.g. fast-forward the local ref to the fetched one when it is an ancestor, or add on the private ref and push it to the role ref), and refuse rather than leave a diverged local note. Test: the other signer pushes a note on a different commit first; --push must land.

F2 MEDIUM worker/board-store.mjs:550-553 (claimReview): "a re-cut keeps the claim" holds only when the other checker passes the right card_id. After a re-cut the new tip is unclaimed, so the dispatch wakes Checker (both sessions run the watch as Checker), and the DISPATCH line names #Cnnn, not the card id. Probe: Checker 2 claims v1 with card_id a-card; Checker claims the re-cut with no card_id -> ok:true; Checker 2 then claims it with card_id -> refused "Checker has this". With card_id b-card -> ok:true as well. claimReview already reads the probe's evidence: derive the card from evidence headSha12 == sha (ignore or check the caller's card_id), so the re-cut rule cannot be skipped. Test: the other checker claims the re-cut with no card_id and with a wrong one; both refused.

F3 LOW collector/store-dispatch.mjs:210: any claim silences the Checker review wake for everyone, the holder too. So Manager's up-front claim never wakes the named checker, and a holder that resets or dies is never re-woken until Manager releases. That is a fair price for one checker per tip, but say it where it is used: the claim_review description (Manager: send the named checker the tip yourself) and the release_review description (a gone holder's tip waits silently until released). Tip with no claim: dispatch unchanged (the card's free/elsewhere tests and my X3 confirm).
